First off, you're already building a profile that is stronger than many people realize.

The military—especially submarines—is highly respected because it demonstrates that you can operate in high-stress, technically demanding, isolated environments. Your background in information systems, cybersecurity, and digital forensics also gives you a strong engineering and analytical foundation.

That said, if your goal is to maximize your chances of becoming an astronaut (whether with NASA or potentially another space agency or commercial company), you'll want to broaden into the sciences and engineering that astronauts use every day.

What NASA Looks For

Most astronaut candidates have several of these:

Master's or Ph.D. in a STEM field
Military service (especially aviation, nuclear, submarines, special operations, engineering)
Leadership experience
Excellent physical fitness
Strong teamwork
Flight experience (optional but valuable)
Research or engineering accomplishments

Your military experience already checks several of those boxes.

