Because you already have experience in networking and information systems, a master's in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, or Aerospace Engineering would complement your existing skills particularly well.

A Multi-Year Study Plan

If I were mentoring someone with your background and long-term goal, I'd suggest this progression:

Year 1: Refresh math through Calculus III, learn Python deeply, and study introductory physics.

Year 2: Work through differential equations, linear algebra, classical mechanics, electricity and magnetism, and begin orbital mechanics.

Year 3: Start your master's program while studying spacecraft systems, propulsion, robotics, and systems engineering. Seek opportunities for research or engineering projects related to aerospace.

Year 4 and beyond: Build specialized expertise, maintain excellent physical fitness, pursue leadership opportunities in the Navy, and, if possible, gain experience in operational environments, research, or aerospace collaborations.

One additional suggestion: because you're in the submarine force, if you have opportunities to qualify as much as possible, take on technical leadership roles, and earn outstanding evaluations, that operational record can be a meaningful asset. Astronaut selection committees value people who have demonstrated exceptional performance in demanding, team-oriented environments.

You're starting from a stronger position than you might think. With your Navy service, an advanced STEM degree, continued technical growth, and leadership experience, you'd be building a profile that aligns well with many of the qualities sought in astronaut candidates.

